An unordered backlog: what is still to do, plus the negative findings that
would otherwise be paid for twice. Completed work is not logged here -
its history lives in docs/plans/, docs/design/, and git.

The ranked survey behind the dbarts-capability items - what the seam is,
what each costs, and which need dbarts to grow first - is
docs/design/dbarts-capability-adoption.md. An item graduates to its own
docs/plans/<item>.md when it is picked up.

items
  plot-method: a plot() method for stan4bartFit, the counterpart of
    dbarts 0.9-x's plot.rbart, which drew two base-graphics panels: the
    residual sd's trace across burn-in and draws, one line per chain,
    and posterior intervals for E(Y|x) against the observed response
    (for a binary response, against the median probability). Scheduled
    after dbarts bartcore merges to main and before the dbarts 1.0-0
    submission (VD 2026-09-24), so it ships in 0.0-14. A short design
    note first: which panels, where the warmup trace comes from given
    save_warmup = FALSE by default (fit$adaptation holds a thinned
    one), what a binary fit shows in place of sigma, and base graphics
    only (no new dependency). residuals() landed alongside the dbarts
    print.rbart/plot.rbart stubs; plot was the one 0.9-x method left.
  callback-estimators: TABLED with its consumer (bartCause) 2026-07-16.
    The design and measurements are complete in
    docs/plans/callback-estimators.md (per-draw reducing callbacks
    store draws x estimators instead of n x draws surfaces; prototype
    +1.1 percent wall, bitwise ate; the stan4bart-side contract
    refinements - assembled response-scale ev to the callback, warmup
    gating, documenting the seam - are consumer-gated and land with
    adoption).
  ordinal-outcomes: ordered-categorical responses via dbarts's
    cumulative probit. Structurally the existing binary path -
    getLatents into the parametric response, cutpoints drawn
    engine-side. Cheapest genuinely new family, and multilevel ordinal
    is a core audience need.
  log-lik-loo: surface dbarts_results.logLikelihood (per-observation,
    already computed for gaussian/binary/aft; the BART offset is the
    parametric mean, so what it reports is the joint conditional
    likelihood) as a log_lik extract type plus loo/waic methods.
    Storage opt-in, same discipline as store=.
  dbarts-passthroughs: wire, document, and test the features that
    dbarts-spec-adoption only made REACHABLE - storage="single", sparse
    columns, warm starts, and the substantive use of monotone and
    interactions()/blocks() (the last is also the cheap R-side
    mitigation of the forest-ranef confounding dbarts investigated and
    declined to fix in the engine). missing="incorporate" (MIA on
    BART-only covariates) is the one item here that is NOT a knob:
    dbartsSpec does not take it, it is a dbartsData argument, and the
    lme4 ingestion in lme4_functions.R drops NA rows through
    model.frame's na.action before dbartsData ever sees them and again
    through the union na.action.all. Reaching it means teaching the
    ingestion to keep NA rows for the bart frame alone while the fixed
    and random frames stay complete - an arc, not a forwarded name.
  per-observation-variance: robust-t residuals and a heteroscedastic
    variance forest, as ONE arc - both need per-observation precision
    to flow BART -> parametric, reversing today's scalar setSigma
    direction. Blocked on dbarts appending a working-weights/variance
    field to dbarts_results (additive, MINOR bump); this side then sets
    the parametric weights per iteration, which the Eigen model already
    carries but fixes at construction. dbarts-gated.
  aft-frailty: multilevel accelerated-failure-time survival.
    dbartsSpec(family = "aft", survival = ) already builds the
    specification; the open question is whether the imputed censored
    response is reachable through getLatents. Check that first - if it
    is, the rest is wiring, and multilevel survival is an unoccupied
    niche.
  multilevel-bcf: the two-forest (prognostic + treatment) BCF
    parameterization under random effects - the largest research payoff
    here, and what bartCause would consume. Needs a new dbarts.h entry
    point: BCF is built by its own path inside dbarts and is not
    exposed through the flat C API at all. decision-gated.
  grouped-fit-wall-time: the four costs behind bartCause's group.by
    route being several times slower than the dbarts route it replaced,
    measured and ranked in docs/design/re-scale-and-grouped-cost.md
    section 5. Ours to move: chain parallelism (a PSOCK/FORK cluster
    with 1.25 s of startup, and cores defaulting to 1, against dbarts'
    free in-engine pthreads - a net LOSS below roughly 2000 iters x 4
    chains), and the counterfactual test surface tripling the fit.
    NOT ours: bartCause forwards neither n.samples nor n.burn nor
    n.chains on this route and never sets cores, so the fit silently
    runs 10 chains at this package's own iter/warmup defaults - 5x the
    work asked for, and the single largest factor. Report that upward.
  gp-leaves-reload: a reload of a keepTrees fit with gp leaves
    (bart_args leaf.prior = gp(columns = )) replays its kept trees about
    0.01 off its stored fits, where every other leaf prior replays to
    1e-10. It predates the one response range per fit.
  ranef-double-bar: extract(type = "ranef") stops ("subscript out of
    bounds") on a fit with a || term.
  one-parameter-binary: a binary fit whose parametric part has one
    parameter, as y ~ x + bart(...), stops when its chains are packaged
    ("'dimnames' applied to non-array").
  test-oracle-old-lme4: the at-home block of
    inst/tinytest/test-02-binary.R calls lme4::glmer itself for its
    deviance comparison. On lme4 1.1-35.5 that call stopped ("Downdated
    VtV is not positive definite") in 4 of 38 one-process runs of the
    suite on one build and fitted in the other 34, on the same data;
    it has not stopped on lme4 2.1.0. The package's own start takes its
    warning and a start of zero when lme4 stops.
  near-constant-target: a response that less its offset is constant up
    to rounding (y = 7 + offset) is given a range a few units in the
    last place wide, and fits with the forest pinned there; a width of
    exactly zero installs no range. No rule says where "no width" ends.
  huge-range-message: a pair, or a response, so large that the
    parametric sampler cannot start (c(1e100, 2e100), a response of the
    order 1e300) stops with the sampler's own words ("inv_mass must be
    in (0, inf).", "non-finite log density or gradient"), not with ones
    that name the range.
  lost-factor-level: a level of a fixed factor that only rows dropped
    for another part's missing value hold stays a level of the fixed
    design. Where it is the factor's first level, or under contrasts
    such as contr.sum, the design keeps a column more than the complete
    rows' and the draws are not theirs: with f on levels a to d, level a
    on row 5 alone and a missing BART predictor on row 5,
    y ~ x + f + (1 | g) + bart(u + v) fits columns fb, fc, fd where the
    data less row 5 fit fc, fd. Under treatment contrasts a lost level
    that is not the first costs lme4's rank message and nothing else.
    Dropping unused levels after the cut in glFormula repairs the fit;
    predict() on new data then has to take its levels from the kept
    rows, where get.orig.levs reads the whole frame, and a new row
    holding the lost level stops as lm's does.
  posterior-nc2-seed: the continuous_nc2 tier of the posterior baseline
    (benchmarks/baselines/MANIFEST, the 83e0c1a row) sits at an outlying
    seed. At its recorded seed the random slope's scale reads 0.975, the
    lowest of 25 seeds on the same data; the other 24 average 1.039 with
    a standard deviation over seeds of 0.017, on the build before the
    one response range as well (1.038, 0.025). A later build whose draws
    shift will read near 1.039 and can fail the tier's mean gate, as the
    comparison against the Stan-era baseline did at 43 of 45, with the
    posterior unmoved. Recording that tier at another seed, or gating a
    tier on several seeds, would end it; neither is decided.
  ppd-zero-weight: extract(type = "ppd") gives NaN, with rnorm's
    warning, on a continuous fit's rows of weight zero.
  ranef-shared-level: the shared level of the group intercepts does not
    mix - lag-1 0.97-0.99 and ESS 1.5-19 per 1000 on every design at
    every skip in benchmarks/baselines/re-scale-BASELINE.csv. It is
    confounded with the forest's overall level, so the two-block
    alternation is what has to cross it and no parametric transition
    can; freezing the forest takes it to lag-1 0.09. Separate from the
    scale and not covered by the dbarts bar. The fix is a joint move
    shifting a scalar between the forest and the intercepts, which
    needs a way to add a constant to every leaf that the dbarts flat C
    API does not expose. docs/plans/re-scale-mixing.md.
  vendored-headers: when syncing a file from dbarts, copy it and
    rewrite external/ -> ext/ (this package names that directory ext,
    dbarts names it external); that one line is the only permitted
    divergence, and vendor only what is reached from this package's own
    sources. As of 412df11 the vendored set is ext/Rinternals.h,
    rc/{bounds,util}.h, rc_{bounds,util}.c - all byte-identical to
    dbarts modulo that rewrite - plus misc/stddef.h, which is the one
    deliberate exception: it omits upstream's misc_index_t, a dbarts
    kernel type this package does not use. Do NOT vendor upstream's
    external/{random,stats}.h or ext/{io,R}.h; io.h declares
    ext_printMessage/ext_throwError/ext_issueWarning, which live in
    dbarts's external.a, a library this package does not link, so the
    header is unusable here rather than merely unused. Do NOT
    re-vendor misc/intrinsic.h or the AX_EXT configure machinery
    deleted with the radix tree - a dbarts source wanting intrinsic.h
    also wants upstream's AX_COMPILER_EXT configure side, and the two
    must move together, since the macro names look cosmetic and are not
    (COMPILER_SUPPORTS_SSE2 asks whether the compiler accepts -msse2;
    the old HAVE_SSE2 asked whether the build host's cpuid reported
    it).
  walnuts-vendoring: the vendored WALNUTS headers carry one hand edit,
    refresh_logp_grad() on AdaptiveWalnuts and WalnutsSampler, kept as
    tools/walnutpie-patches/0001-refresh-logp-grad.patch and reapplied by
    tools/vendor-walnutpie.sh on every re-vendoring. It exists because the
    upstream sampler caches the log density and gradient at its current
    position across transitions and this package moves the target
    between them; without it the chain is biased, not merely slower.
    The maintainer intends to raise it upstream; if it lands there, drop
    the patch and the mention of it in inst/include/WALNUTPIE_VERSION.
    Update with tools/vendor-walnutpie.sh <sha>, then reread the API
    drift and numerics notes in WALNUTPIE_VERSION by hand - the script
    rewrites the provenance lines and nothing else.
  perf-measurement: two traps, kept because both were expensive to find
    and neither leaves a trace once the wrong number is written down.
    (1) Per-iteration cost must be differenced between two fits IN ONE
    WARM PROCESS. Differencing two cold subprocesses charges the long
    run for page-in on the sample-storage arrays, a cost that scales
    with iter and reads back as compute. The error is not a constant
    offset - it grows with the per-draw storage footprint, so it does
    not cancel across commits: it reported 700 us/iter on 174b369
    against a warm truth of 163, and 137 on 3e2036b against 144. That
    gradient was the whole of a "4x per-iteration speedup" chased
    across 174b369..3e2036b, where cost is in fact flat; no stan4bart
    or dbarts commit in that window moved it. Fixed in bench-perf.R at
    412df11, whose comment carries the numbers.
    (2) Quiet is top's INSTANTANEOUS idle sample. Load average and ps
    %CPU both decay, so they read a just-finished benchmark as load and
    a genuinely idle machine as busy.
    (3) Compare two samplers only with dbarts pinned to ONE version.
    Neither side can run against both - the pre-merge line predates the
    parsePriors and structSize changes 1.0-0 requires - so a rebuild of
    the older side against the older dbarts is the only honest pairing.
    This is how NEWS.md's 53-95x was derived; see the cb809b1 MANIFEST
    row.
  predict-weights-deparse: predict(fit, newdata) errors in
    getTestDataFrames ("'length = 26' in coercion to 'logical(1)'") when
    the fit's call holds the weights as a value, not a name.
  extract-test-no-test-data: extract and fitted of "ev" and "indiv.fixef"
    at sample = "test" on a fit with no test data say "newdata has
    unrecognized fixef names"; "indiv.bart" returns without error.
  test-cores-literal: inst/tinytest/test-01-continuous.R passes a literal
    cores = 4 inside its at_home block; tests pass at most 2 cores (the
    machine is shared with other jobs). Before the release.
  release: stan4bart 0.0-14, in lockstep with dbarts 1.0-0. dbarts
    ships first; its TODO owns the ordered procedure. LAST.
